1. React Frontend (User Interaction Layer)
What this is:
The React Frontend is what the user sees and interacts with in the browser. It is built with React, Vite, and Material UI, and uses Context for state management.
Key responsibilities:
•	Display the UI (login, items list, item detail, chat)
•	Manage state using: 
o	AuthContext (user + token)
o	Items context (item data)
o	Chat context (real-time messaging)
From here, all actions start.
________________________________________
2. Authentication Flow (Bearer Token)
Flow:
1.	The user logs in or registers.
2.	The backend returns a session token.
3.	The frontend stores this token.
4.	For protected actions, the frontend sends requests with: 
5.	Authorization: Bearer <token>
Why this matters:
This ensures that only authenticated users can:
•	Create items
•	Edit or delete their own items
•	Access chat features
This is shown in the diagram as the “Auth header – Authorization: Bearer token” flowing from the frontend to the backend.
________________________________________
3. REST API Flow (Standard Requests)
Flow:
•	The React Frontend sends REST JSON requests to the Node.js server.
Examples of REST endpoints:
•	/auth/* – login, register, me, logout
•	/items – create, read, update, delete items
•	/chat/:itemId/messages – fetch chat history
What happens:
•	Requests go to the Node.js Server
•	Express routes pass the request to controllers
•	Controllers apply business logic and validation
This is represented by the REST JSON arrows in the diagram.
________________________________________
4. Node.js Server (Express + Socket.IO)
Important clarification (final version):
The diagram correctly shows that Express and Socket.IO run in the same Node.js HTTP server, not in separate servers.
The server does two jobs:
1.	Express API – handles REST requests
2.	Socket.IO – handles real-time messaging
This design keeps the architecture simpler and more efficient.
________________________________________
5. Sequelize ORM Layer (Data Access)
What this is:
Sequelize is the Object Relational Mapping layer that sits between the backend and the database.
Models shown in the diagram:
•	User
•	Session
•	Item
•	ChatMessage
Flow:
•	Express controllers and Socket.IO handlers use Sequelize models.
•	Sequelize translates JavaScript operations into SQL queries.
•	Queries are sent to MySQL.
This is shown as Read / Write SQL Queries from Sequelize to MySQL.
________________________________________
6. MySQL Database (Persistence Layer)
What is stored here:
•	Users and their credentials (hashed passwords)
•	Sessions (login tokens and expiry)
•	Items (donation listings)
•	Chat messages (linked to items and users)
Why it’s important:
•	All application state is persisted.
•	Chat messages remain available even after page refresh or reconnect.
________________________________________
7. Real Time Chat Flow (Socket.IO)
Flow:
1.	The frontend connects to Socket.IO.
2.	The user joins an item specific room (joinItemRoom).
3.	Messages are sent using sendMessage.
4.	The server: 
o	Saves the message to the database via Sequelize.
o	Emits newMessage to all users in the room.
Purpose:
•	Enables instant messaging without page refresh.
•	Keeps chat conversations scoped to a specific item.
This is why chat is shown as a separate real time flow, not part of REST.
________________________________________
8. Image Upload & Serving Flow
Upload flow:
1.	The user uploads an item image.
2.	The image is sent to the backend.
3.	The Node.js server saves the file to the /uploads folder.
4.	The image file path is stored in the database.
Serve flow:
•	The frontend requests images via: 
•	GET /uploads/<filename>
•	The server serves the static image file.
This is why the /uploads folder is shown as a separate file storage component in the diagram.
________________________________________
9. Overall End to End Flow Summary
In simple terms:
1.	The user interacts with the React frontend.
2.	REST requests and Socket.IO events are sent to the Node.js server.
3.	The server processes logic using MVC controllers and Socket.IO handlers.
4.	Data is stored and retrieved using Sequelize and MySQL.
5.	Images are handled via the uploads folder.
6.	Responses, data, images, and real time updates flow back to the frontend.
