Here’s a single paragraph you can read out loud for ~4 minutes that explains the architecture and end‑to‑end flow of DONaid, aligned with what you built (React + MUI frontend, Express MVC backend, Sequelize/MySQL persistence, token auth, uploads for images, Socket.IO real‑time chat). [DONaid Pro...esentation | PowerPoint], [DONaid Pro...esentation | PowerPoint], [DONaid Pro...esentation | PowerPoint]
DONaid follows a clear client–server architecture where the React frontend is responsible for the user interface and user interactions, and the Node.js backend provides the business logic, data persistence, and real‑time messaging.  On the client side, the user opens the application and interacts with pages such as login, sign up, the items list, and the item detail view; the UI is built with React and Material UI, and state is coordinated using Context so authentication state, items data, and chat behavior remain consistent across the app.  The first part of the flow is authentication: when a user registers, the backend creates a new user record and stores the password securely as a salted hash, and when the user logs in successfully, the backend generates a session token and returns it to the frontend.  From that point onward, the frontend attaches that token on protected requests using the HTTP header format Authorization: Bearer <token>, and the backend validates that token against a session record before allowing access to protected endpoints.  The “standard” application operations use REST JSON calls from the frontend to the Express API: for example, fetching the items list, opening a specific item detail, creating an item, updating it, deleting it, and retrieving historical chat messages for an item all run as REST requests handled by Express routes and controllers in an MVC structure.  Inside the backend, Express routes forward requests to controllers, controllers apply validation and business rules, and then data operations are executed through Sequelize models, which act as the ORM layer that translates JavaScript operations into SQL queries for MySQL.  In terms of data design, the database is centered around four core entities: Users represent the accounts, Sessions store session tokens and expiry details for logged-in users, Items store donation listings and link each listing to its owner, and ChatMessages store item-based messages linked to both a specific item and the sending user; this relationship structure ensures that ownership rules and item-specific conversations are enforceable at the database level and consistent across the application.  The item listing flow is a good example of the end-to-end pipeline: when a logged-in user submits the “add item” form, the frontend sends a POST request to the items endpoint with the item details and the authentication token; the controller verifies the token, confirms the request is valid, and creates the item record associated with the authenticated user.  If the item includes an image, the backend saves the file into an uploads directory on the server and stores the filename or path in the database; later, when the items list or detail page renders, the frontend retrieves the image via a standard HTTP static file request like GET /uploads/<filename>, so images load efficiently without embedding raw image bytes in every API response.  For update and delete, the backend enforces an important business rule: only the item owner can edit or remove the listing; the frontend surfaces edit/delete controls only for the owner, and the backend also checks ownership server-side, returning an authorization error if a non-owner attempts to modify the item.  The real-time communication flow is handled separately from REST because chat needs low-latency updates: when a user opens the item detail page and starts a conversation, the frontend connects to Socket.IO, joins an item-specific “room,” and sends messages as Socket.IO events; the backend Socket.IO handler receives the event, persists the message to MySQL through Sequelize so the conversation is not lost on refresh, and then broadcasts a “new message” event to other connected clients in the same room so they see the message instantly.  At the same time, the app also supports reliability through REST-based chat history: when the item detail page loads, the frontend can fetch existing messages using a REST history endpoint, and Socket.IO handles only the new live messages after that, combining persistence with real-time responsiveness.  Finally, the architecture is supported by a testing strategy that validates the backend behavior and business rules: automated tests using Jest and Supertest confirm that authentication works, CRUD operations behave correctly, and owner-only permissions are enforced; coverage reporting then provides evidence of test execution and helps identify untested branches, which strengthens confidence in correctness and maintainability.  In summary, the flow is: the React UI collects user actions, REST endpoints handle structured data operations, Socket.IO handles real-time chat events, Sequelize provides consistent data access, MySQL stores the persistent system state, and uploads provide file storage for images—together delivering a complete donation workflow from sign up to listing, discovery, and coordinated messaging.


